    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nThe `ipv6 enable` command in Cisco enables IPv6 processing on an interface. In Juniper, the equivalent is configuring the `family inet6` under the interface unit configuration, which enables IPv6 support for that interface. I've included both interface configurations since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nThe `ipv6 enable` command in Cisco enables IPv6 processing on an interface. In Juniper, the equivalent is configuring the `family inet6` under the interface unit configuration, which enables IPv6 support for that interface. I've included both interface configurations since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:101::2/96', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:101::2/96\\n##\\n\\nThis command:\\n1. Selects the GigabitEthernet1/0/0 interface (ge-1/0/0 in JunOS)\\n2. Configures unit 0 (logical interface, required in JunOS)\\n3. Enables IPv6 address family\\n4. Assigns the specified IPv6 address with /96 prefix\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:101::2/96'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Cisco \"ipv6 enable\" command enables IPv6 processing on an interface. In Juniper, this is equivalent to configuring the \"family inet6\" under the interface\\'s unit configuration, which enables IPv6 support for that interface. I included commands for both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) from the original configuration since they both had \"ipv6 enable\".', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Cisco \"ipv6 enable\" command enables IPv6 processing on an interface. In Juniper, this is equivalent to configuring the \"family inet6\" under the interface\\'s unit configuration, which enables IPv6 support for that interface. I included commands for both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) from the original configuration since they both had \"ipv6 enable\".', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
